<section>
    <h2>Why tests flake</h2>
    <p>
        A flaky test passes and fails on the same code. Almost always, it's a race: the test and the page disagree
        about <em>when</em>. The test clicks before the button exists, reads text before it changes, holds on to an
        element the page has already replaced, or sleeps for a guess that's right on your laptop and wrong on CI.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Fixed sleeps.</strong> Too short sometimes, too slow always.</li>
        <li><strong>Reading, then asserting.</strong> <code>TextContentAsync()</code> checks one instant.</li>
        <li><strong>Stale elements.</strong> An element handle points at one node; re-renders replace it.</li>
        <li><strong>Things in the way.</strong> Overlays, spinners and animations that catch the click.</li>
        <li><strong>Shared state.</strong> Tests that depend on each other's data or order (floor 16).</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Waiting for the right thing</h2>
<pre><code>// Flaky
await Page.WaitForTimeoutAsync(2000);
var text = await Page.Locator(".status").TextContentAsync();
Assert.That(text, Is.EqualTo("Saved"));

// Steady: the assertion retries until it's true (or times out)
await Expect(Page.GetByRole(AriaRole.Status)).ToHaveTextAsync("Saved");

// Read a value only once it's the one you want
await Expect(total).ToHaveTextAsync(new Regex(@@"^Total: \d+"));
var value = await total.TextContentAsync();</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Locators are lazy.</strong> A locator finds its element again every time you use it, so a re-render
        doesn't break it. Actions wait for the element to be attached, visible, stable, enabled and not covered by
        anything else, then act.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Hunting a flake</h2>
<pre><code>[Test, Repeat(20)]                // run it 20 times in a row; one failure fails it
public async Task SavesTheDraft() { ... }</code></pre>
    <p>
        When it fails, look at the trace (turn tracing on in the test's context) and see what the page actually
        looked like at the moment the test acted. <code>[Retry(2)]</code> can keep a pipeline green while you hunt,
        but a retried pass is still a flake: fix the race, then take the retry away.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>The Flake</h2>
    <p>
        The last monster. Three rounds, one breath: its Strike appears late and hides behind a mist, it flickers and
        feints before it gives its true name, and the box you speak it into is rebuilt under your fingers. Every
        trick a flaky test falls for, all at once. Twenty-four seconds.
    </p>
</section>
